Three files is workable, and the missing gold standard changes the design less than you might think. Two adjustments:

Bucketing comes from metadata and process linkage instead of SME buckets. Candidates: same CTRL_CATEGORY, same COMMON_CTRL_TYP, or linked to the same process or parent process. At 7,500 controls, full pairwise cosine is also fine in memory, so buckets become a scoring feature rather than a hard constraint.
The CT1 to CT28 payments mapping becomes your evaluation slice. It covers a subset, but it is real SME judgement. Run the pipeline over all 7,500, then measure precision and recall on the payments controls where the answer is known. That gives a number without waiting for new SME reviews.

What I need from you to start

Column metadata for both files. Column name, data type, a one-line description, and ideally fill rate (percentage non-blank). For text columns, a rough sense of typical length helps.
Which columns are free text versus coded. Title, description, objective, "how it operates" and similar are what get embedded. Everything coded is for scoring and slicing.
Structure of the linkage file. Column names, whether a control can link to many processes, whether a link type or strength field exists, and roughly what proportion of controls have at least one link.
Whether processes have a hierarchy. Parent process ID or level field. Same-parent linkage is a useful duplicate signal.
A handful of example rows, masked or paraphrased if the real text cannot leave the environment. Ten rows per file is enough to see how the text is written and whether IDs leak into descriptions.
The CT1 to CT28 mapping file structure, so I can design the evaluation join.
Confirmation of where the code runs. I assume you run Python locally with the OpenAI key and I write the code; if so, tell me your Python version and whether pandas, scikit-learn and openai are already installed.
